Ah yes, you are correct you need to add a null terminator to buffer manually before calling `strcmp` (or call `strncmp`).

And I think I got the wrong operator (sorry I didn't debug it).
I *think* this might work (again, do debug, easy for me to mess up lol :grin: ):

```
	const char *sample = "version https://git-lfs";
	int l = strlen(sample);

	const PackedByteArray buffer = file->get_buffer(l);

	// Check all the bytes required,
	// and only check the number of active characters
	// because buffer doesn't include NULL terminator.
	bool match = ((buffer.size() == l) && (!strncmp(sample, (const char*)buffer.ptr(), l)));
```

The C `strcmp` etc functions are notoriously easy to get wrong (and have buffer overflows etc) that's why they are the site of many security exploits (and why they are often discouraged). Nearly every time I've used them I've created some kind of bug :grinning: .
